Experimental physics and instrumentation (@experimental-physics-and-instrumentation: 0, is #chapter, #outline) { r_6B235FF0NQSETNXHKF6HTVYN2E
Experimental physics designs observations that test physical explanations and reveal unknown behaviour. This material connects apparatus, detectors, vacuum, cryogenics, electronics, timing, data acquisition, and analysis with calibration and systematic-error studies, exposing the infrastructure needed to make convincing physical measurements.

**Common preparation developed:** the [quantum observation comparison](../core/Compare-quantum-predictions-with-counts-losses-and-alternative-preparation-accounts.lingua) separates model probabilities from counts, postselection and detector losses. Two distinct preparation/efficiency accounts give the same complete recorded law. Independent calibration and discriminating evidence remain required; no laboratory competence, apparatus or empirical dataset replication is claimed.

**Parent:** [Physics — fields, matter, motion and physical evidence](../Physics.lingua#r_04A5CP8RTBERZEN4XCCDQ3T47W).
} r_6B235FF0NQSETNXHKF6HTVYN2E
